---
export const title = 'Usage';
export const order = 100;
import DemoComponentCard from '../../../../components/DemoComponentCard.astro';
---

<p>
  <code>wc-password-rules</code> is a checklist under a new-password field. Point <code>for</code> at
  the field's id: the list ticks each requirement off as the person types, and while a typed password
  fails a rule the field is invalid ("Password does not meet the requirements."), so the form does
  not submit. An empty field is left to its own <code>required</code>.
</p>

<DemoComponentCard lang={'html'}>
  <div style='width: 100%; max-width: 320px; display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 0.5rem;'>
    <wc-password-field
      id='password-rules-demo-field'
      label='New password'
      autocomplete='new-password'
      required
    ></wc-password-field>
    <wc-password-rules
      id='password-rules-demo'
      for='password-rules-demo-field'
      minlength='12'
      require-uppercase
      require-lowercase
      require-number
      require-symbol
    ></wc-password-rules>
  </div>
  <script is:inline>
    document.querySelector('#password-rules-demo').addEventListener('change', function (event) {
      myConsole.log('change: valid=' + event.detail.valid + ' failed=' + event.detail.failed.join(','));
    });
  </script>
</DemoComponentCard>

<h3>Accessibility</h3>
<p>
  Each requirement shows its state with an icon shape (a tick or an empty circle), never color alone,
  and screen readers hear "met" or "not met" after it. Changes are announced politely once typing
  pauses for a second, and only for the requirements whose state flipped; when the last one is met,
  "All password requirements met" is announced instead.
</p>
